        difficulty: { level: 'Very high', text: "Differential gears are forged and machined to tight tolerances for global makers that audit every step, and motors and controllers need electrical design and software. Sona developed 19 products in-house, and Denso agreeing to pay it royalties for two- and three-wheeler motor technology is, management believes, a first for an Indian parts maker." },
        moat: "Owned designs, a record with global makers and a 69-programme EV pipeline make it hard to displace once designed in. The weakness: the newer motor and railway businesses earn less than gears, so growth lowers the margin, and part of the best-growing line is going to Denso.",
        salesDrivers: ["**Order book**: ₹24,000cr, 5.4 times FY26 revenue; ₹15,400cr is electric, and 29 of its 69 EV programmes are not yet in production.", "**Electric and hybrid content**: battery-electric revenue more than doubled to ₹436cr in Q1 after a weak Q1 FY26 (rare-earth magnet curbs, US tariffs). Suspension motors are the fastest-growing product.", "**Denso partnerships**: a joint venture for high-voltage inverters, motors and generators for cars and larger vehicles, in which Denso holds 51% and licenses its technology, aimed at an Indian hybrid and EV market S&P Global Mobility sizes at about ₹24,000cr in 2030; and the sale of 49% of the two- and three-wheeler motor business to Denso at a ₹1,750cr enterprise value, with royalties to Sona.", "**Capex**: FY26 capex was ₹369cr plus ₹110cr of land. Motors need little plant: ₹8–12 of revenue per ₹1 of capex. Management says customers' supply chains, not its capacity, limit growth.", "**Guidance**: margin band 23–25%, with recovery ‘from Q2 onwards’; no revenue guidance, but an ambition to grow about tenfold over the next decade, including robotics."],
        inputs: "**Alloy steel** for forged gears, and **copper wire, rare-earth magnets and electronic chips** for motors and controllers. Rare-earth magnets are the strong permanent magnets inside most electric motors, made from metals that China dominates; from April 2025 China required export licences for them, which cut Sona's Q1 FY26 electric revenue. Sona switched to ‘light’ rare-earth magnets five quarters ago and says it now sees no shortage; India approved a ₹7,280cr scheme in November 2025 for 6,000 tonnes a year of local magnets, two to three years away. Materials were 47% of sales in FY26.",
        costEffect: "**Partly passed on, with a lag.** Commodity costs are passed through to customers after a delay; wage rises (Haryana's minimum wage from April 2026) and a gas shortage are not. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 5 points (24% to 19%). **What happened:** there is no single price index for its mix of steel, magnets, copper and chips, so there is no input chart. The margin fell from 28% in FY24 to 27% in FY25 and 24% in FY26, mostly because lower-margin motors and railway equipment grew faster than gears, not because of input prices. Quarterly it ran 24%, 25%, 25% and 24% through FY26 and 23% in Q1 FY27 (company: 23.1%), when ‘April was tough, May was tougher’.",
